{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-162397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\,1-t⟩$ at $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3},\\,y(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3{t}^{2},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =6t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|3{t}^{2}\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 6t|}{{((3t)^{2}+(1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6|t|}{{(9{t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{6|1|}{{(9(1)^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{(10)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{1}{κ(1)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "74e14b9f7c75ded94667306b108c8a7ac36812dc60420792697d99fe4dc65328"} {"question" "q-162397" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162397" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162397" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+(1-t)\\,j$ at $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,y(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2t\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2|}{{((2t)^{2}+(-1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{{(4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "74e14b9f7c75ded94667306b108c8a7ac36812dc60420792697d99fe4dc65328"} {"question" "q-162404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=(1+t)^{2}\\,i+(1-t)^{2}\\,j$ at $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1+t)^{2},\\,y(t)=(1-t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2(1+t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2(1-t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2(1+t)\\cdot 2-(-2(1-t))\\cdot 2|}{{(\\,(2(1+t))^{2}+(2(1-t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{(8+8{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{8}{{(8+8(0)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{(8+0)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "74e14b9f7c75ded94667306b108c8a7ac36812dc60420792697d99fe4dc65328"} {"question" "q-162405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt[3]{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt[3]{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt[3]{4}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ at $x=\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{[1+{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{{[1+{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{2}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "74e14b9f7c75ded94667306b108c8a7ac36812dc60420792697d99fe4dc65328"} {"question" "q-162532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=x-{e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=1-{e}^{x},\\,{y}^{″}(x)=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{-{e}^{x}}{{[1+{(1-{e}^{x})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{(1+1+{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{x})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{(2+{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{x})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(1) & =-\\frac{{e}^{(1)}}{{(2+{e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{(1)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "74e14b9f7c75ded94667306b108c8a7ac36812dc60420792697d99fe4dc65328"} {"question" "q-162535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-e+1)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-e+2)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-e+2)^{3/2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"} {"question" "q-162535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072275/state.json" "file_sha256" "a2befd06bae7a5b4753c2352a32290d77dc073a96b14ede01c9db34c4e124a6e"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2626 "retractions" [] "i